//   Bun's native Bun.spawnSync uses a different path, so we prefer it and fall back to execFileSync
//   only under plain Node (some test runners).
//
//   PART 2 (2026-07-01): a long-lived daemon (hours of Bun.serve + WASM + repeated spawns) degrades
//   into a state where Bun.spawnSync with PIPED stdio spuriously fast-fails on repo-access git --
//   success:false, signalCode set, EMPTY stderr, in ~50ms (NOT the timeout). The `?deep=1` health
//   probe survives the SAME corrupted process only because it uses stdout:'ignore' (no parent-side
//   pipe read). Repro'd only in the aged daemon instance; a restart clears it; seven controlled
//   fresh-process variables (WASM mem/file, handle count, env-reconstruct, hidden/detached launch,
//   timeout-poison) do NOT reproduce it. The structural dodge: never make the parent read a pipe.
//   Redirect the child's stdout/stderr to temp FILES (fd-backed, like 'ignore'), then read the
//   files back. This makes git work even inside the corrupted process, so a wedged daemon self-heals
//   on the next sync instead of silently starving the brain of new content until a manual restart.
function gitSpawnBun(B: BunSpawn, argv: string[], gitEnv: NodeJS.ProcessEnv): string {
  const base = join(tmpdir(), `gbrain-git-${process.pid}-${gitSpawnSeq++}`);
  const outP = `${base}.out`;
  const errP = `${base}.err`;
